values
  ('G9-NUM-REAL-IRRATIONAL', 'Real numbers: rational or irrational', 'By the end of this lesson, you should be able to classify real numbers as rational or irrational.', 'Rational numbers can be written as a fraction of two integers, with a non-zero denominator. Integers, terminating decimals and recurring decimals are rational. For a non-negative integer radicand, its square root is rational exactly when that integer is a perfect square. More generally, rational radicands can also have rational roots: √(1/4) = 1/2. Numbers such as √2 and π are real but irrational.', 'Classify √81.
1. 81 is a non-negative integer and a perfect square: 9 × 9 = 81.
2. √81 = 9.
3. 9 is an integer, so it is rational.
Also, √(1/4) = 1/2, showing that a rational non-integer radicand can have a rational square root.
Therefore √81 is a rational real number.', 'A root sign does not automatically mean “irrational”. For an integer inside √, check whether it is a perfect square; for fractions, simplify the root carefully.'),
  ('G9-NUM-WHOLE-HCF-LCM', 'Factors, multiples, HCF and LCM', 'By the end of this lesson, you should be able to use prime factors to find an HCF or LCM.', 'A factor divides a number exactly; a multiple is made by multiplying. Prime factorisation writes a number as primes. For HCF use only common prime factors at their smallest powers. For LCM use every required prime factor at its largest power.', 'Find the HCF and LCM of 24 and 36.
24 = 2³ × 3 and 36 = 2² × 3².
HCF = 2² × 3 = 12.
